<!-- Section 1: sec-intro -->
<section id="sec-intro" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Projection Q Calculator</h2>
  <p>This calculator finds projection q of a right triangle when you know leg b and the complete hypotenuse c. Projection q is the segment of the hypotenuse that sits next to leg b, created when the altitude from the right angle meets the hypotenuse. The formula is <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{b^2}{c}]]')} />, where b is the perpendicular leg and c is the full hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>For example, with <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, the calculation is <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{8^2}{10} = \\frac{64}{10} = 6.4]]')} /> cm. The value 6.4 cm is the portion of the hypotenuse associated with leg b, not the leg itself and not the whole hypotenuse.</p>
  <p>The calculator sits at the top of this page, followed by the formula, a labeled diagram, a proof, worked examples, and checks you can run on any answer.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: sec-calculate -->
<section id="sec-calculate" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Calculate Right Triangle Projection Q</h2>
  <p>The calculator has two input fields. The first takes leg b, the perpendicular side associated with projection q. The second takes the hypotenuse c, the complete side opposite the right angle. Both lengths must use the same unit, so convert first if one measurement is in centimeters and the other is in meters.</p>
  <p>The hypotenuse must be longer than leg b, since a leg is one side of the right angle while the hypotenuse spans the whole triangle. If your b value is larger than c, you have probably entered a projection, the altitude, or the wrong side.</p>
  <p>Click Calculate Projection q to see the result, and use Reset to clear both fields before a new problem. The result is a length in the unit you entered, so a leg of 8 cm and a hypotenuse of 10 cm return 6.4 cm. The result is a segment length along the hypotenuse.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: sec-what-projection-q -->
<section id="sec-what-projection-q" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>What Is Projection Q in a Right Triangle?</h2>
  <p>Draw the altitude from the right-angle vertex C perpendicular to the hypotenuse AB. It meets the hypotenuse at a point called the foot, labeled D, and splits it into two adjacent segments: AD is projection p, and DB is projection q.</p>
  <p>Projection q belongs to leg b because it sits next to vertex B, the same vertex where leg b begins. In the small triangle CBD, leg b is the hypotenuse and q is one of its legs, which is the geometric link that makes the projection theorem work. Projection p, by the same reasoning, belongs to leg a, which touches vertex A.</p>
  <p>Projections are lengths along the hypotenuse, measured in the same unit as the other sides. They are not perpendicular to anything, and they are not sides of the original triangle. The <a href="/right-triangle-projection-calculator/">right triangle projection calculator</a> finds both projections when the legs and hypotenuse are known.</p>
  <p>Because p and q are the two parts of one straight side, they add up to the whole hypotenuse:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[p + q = c]]]')} /></p>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 6.4]]')} /> cm, the other projection is <span set:html={rich('[[p = 10 - 6.4 = 3.6]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Projection q is not the same as leg b. Leg b is side BC, one of the sides forming the right angle, while q is segment DB along the hypotenuse. In the 6-8-10 triangle, leg b is 8 cm and projection q is only 6.4 cm.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: sec-leg-b-hypotenuse -->
<section id="sec-leg-b-hypotenuse" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Understanding Leg B and the Hypotenuse</h2>
  <p>A right triangle has two perpendicular sides, called legs, and one longest side, called the hypotenuse. The two legs meet at the right angle, and the hypotenuse sits opposite that angle. Here the triangle is ABC with the right angle at C: leg a is AC, leg b is BC, and the hypotenuse c is AB.</p>
  <p>Leg b is one of the two sides that form the right angle, so it is a genuine side of the triangle, not a segment drawn inside it. The projection q lies flat along the hypotenuse, while leg b runs from vertex B to the right-angle vertex C.</p>
  <p>The hypotenuse is always the longest side in a nondegenerate right triangle, since it sits opposite the largest angle, the right angle. That is why the formula requires <span set:html={rich('[[0 < b < c]]')} /> and why the calculator expects a hypotenuse longer than the leg.</p>
  <p>The position of leg b decides which segment is called q: leg b touches vertex B, so the segment next to B is q. Always check the labels on your own diagram before choosing which leg to enter.</p>
