<!-- Section 5: sec-formula -->
<section id="sec-formula" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Projection Q Formula</h2>
  <p>The projection theorem states that the square of leg b equals the hypotenuse multiplied by its projection:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b^2 = c \\times q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Dividing both sides by c isolates q and gives the working formula for this page:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[q = \\frac{b^2}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>The calculation runs in two stages. Stage 1 squares leg b, multiplying it by itself. Stage 2 divides that squared length by the complete hypotenuse c, and the result is an ordinary length, not a squared length.</p>
  <p>Units need care at both stages. If b and c are both in centimeters, then <span set:html={rich('[[b^2]]')} /> is in square centimeters, and dividing by centimeters returns centimeters. Mixing units, such as meters for b and centimeters for c, gives a meaningless quotient, so convert to one unit before calculating.</p>
  <p>With <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, stage 1 gives <span set:html={rich('[[8^2 = 64]]')} />, and stage 2 gives <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{64}{10} = 6.4]]')} /> cm. The number 64 answers no question on its own: only after the division does it become the projection length.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('projection-q-formula.svg')} alt="Right triangle ABC with altitude CD, showing projection q equals leg b squared divided by the hypotenuse c" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">The projection formula: q equals b squared divided by c, with the altitude foot D splitting the hypotenuse.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: sec-why-squared -->
<section id="sec-why-squared" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Is Leg B Squared in the Projection Formula?</h2>
  <p>The square is there because the projection theorem is a statement about a squared leg: it says the square of leg b equals the product of c and q, <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = c \\times q]]')} />, and the formula for q is what you get by dividing both sides by c.</p>
  <p>The reason traces back to similar triangles. In triangle CBD, leg b plays the role of the hypotenuse, while q is one of its legs. Comparing the small triangle with the whole triangle gives the proportion <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{q}{b} = \\frac{b}{c}]]')} />, and leg b appears twice, once in each ratio. Cross-multiplying puts b on both sides, which is exactly why the square appears.</p>
  <p>The geometric mean relationship says the same thing in different words: leg b is the geometric mean of the hypotenuse c and its projection q, meaning <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\sqrt{c \\times q}]]')} />. Squaring both sides gives <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = c \\times q]]')} />, and dividing by c isolates q.</p>
  <p>Division is the step that isolates q. Multiplication in the theorem binds q to c, and dividing by c undoes that binding. Without the square, the formula would mix a length with an area, and the units would not work out.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 7: sec-how-to-use -->
<section id="sec-how-to-use" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Use the Right Triangle Projection Q Calculator</h2>
  <p>Eleven short steps take you from a labeled diagram to a checked answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Identify the right triangle in your problem and confirm it has a right angle.</li>
    <li>Locate the right-angle vertex and the altitude from it to the hypotenuse.</li>
    <li>Identify the perpendicular leg associated with projection q, the leg touching vertex B.</li>
    <li>Identify the complete hypotenuse c, the side opposite the right angle.</li>
    <li>Confirm c is greater than b. If it is not, relabel the diagram before continuing.</li>
    <li>Select compatible units and convert if the measurements use different units.</li>
    <li>Enter leg b in the Leg b field.</li>
    <li>Enter hypotenuse c in the Hypotenuse c field.</li>
    <li>Click Calculate Projection q and read the result.</li>
    <li>Check the result: q must be positive and smaller than c.</li>
    <li>Use Reset when starting a new calculation.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Entering leg a instead of leg b produces a different projection. Leg a pairs with p, so the calculator would return the segment next to vertex A, not the one next to B. The label check in step 3 matters most.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 8: sec-step-example -->
<section id="sec-step-example" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Example of Finding Projection Q</h2>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm. The four steps below show the full working, with nothing skipped.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write the formula: <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{b^2}{c}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Substitute the values: <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{8^2}{10}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Square the leg: <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{64}{10}]]')} />.</li>
    <li>Divide: <span set:html={rich('[[q = 6.4]]')} /> cm.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The answer 6.4 cm is the segment of the hypotenuse next to leg b, not a perpendicular side and not an area. A short verification closes the loop: <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = 8^2 = 64]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c \\times q = 10 \\times 6.4 = 64]]')} />, so both sides of the theorem agree.</p>
  <p>For reference, the other projection is <span set:html={rich('[[p = 10 - 6.4 = 3.6]]')} /> cm, leg a is <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{10 \\times 3.6} = 6]]')} /> cm, and the altitude is <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\sqrt{3.6 \\times 6.4} = 4.8]]')} /> cm, confirming the familiar 6-8-10 right triangle.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('projection-q-example-b8-c10.svg')} alt="Right triangle with leg b 8 cm and hypotenuse 10 cm, worked to give projection q equals 6.4 cm" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Worked example: b = 8 cm and c = 10 cm give q = 64 divided by 10 = 6.4 cm.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 9: sec-diagram -->
<section id="sec-diagram" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Right Triangle Projection Q Diagram Explained</h2>
  <p>The main diagram shows triangle ABC with the right angle at C. Side AC is leg a, side BC is leg b, and side AB is the hypotenuse c. Altitude CD drops from C perpendicular to AB and touches it at D.</p>
  <p>Point D splits the hypotenuse into two parts: segment AD is projection p and segment DB is projection q. Leg b and projection q are highlighted because they are the pair this page is about. Trace the labels in this order: start at B, run along leg b to C, then along leg a to A, then back along the hypotenuse from A to B, pausing at D to see the split into p and q.</p>
  <p>The pairing of b with q becomes visible in the small triangle CBD, where leg b is the hypotenuse and q is the side lying along the big hypotenuse. Because triangle CBD is similar to triangle ABC, the ratio of q to b in the small triangle equals the ratio of b to c in the big one.</p>
