<!-- Section 10: sec-proof -->
<section id="sec-proof" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Why Does the Projection Q Formula Work?</h2>
  <p>The proof rests on similar triangles. Drawing altitude CD divides triangle ABC into two smaller right triangles, ACD and CBD, and both are similar to the original triangle. One of their side ratios gives the projection theorem directly.</p>
  <p>Focus on triangle CBD and the whole triangle ABC. Both contain the angle at B, and both contain a right angle, so their third angles must match as well. Matching the sides that sit in the same positions gives the proportion:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{DB}{BC} = \\frac{BC}{AB}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Now substitute the standard symbols: <span set:html={rich('[[DB = q]]')} />, <span set:html={rich('[[BC = b]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[AB = c]]')} />. The proportion becomes:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[\\frac{q}{b} = \\frac{b}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Cross-multiplying clears the fractions. The left numerator times the right denominator equals the right numerator times the left denominator, so <span set:html={rich('[[q \\times c = b \\times b]]')} />, which is:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b^2 = c \\times q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Dividing both sides by c finishes the proof, giving <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{b^2}{c}]]')} />. The same argument applied to the other small triangle, ACD, gives <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = c \\times p]]')} />.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('projection-q-similar-triangles.svg')} alt="Original right triangle ABC beside smaller similar triangles ACD and CBD, showing the proportion q over b equals b over c" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Similar-triangles proof: q over b equals b over c, so cross-multiplying gives b squared equals c times q.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 11: sec-examples -->
<section id="sec-examples" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Worked Examples of Calculating Projection Q</h2>
  <p>Each example states the given values, the substitution, and the final answer, and every example keeps its units consistent from start to finish.</p>
  <h3>Example 1: Basic Whole Numbers</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, the formula gives <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{8^2}{10} = \\frac{64}{10}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[q = 6.4]]')} /> cm. The squared leg 64 is an intermediate value, and dividing by the hypotenuse turns it back into a length. The other leg of this triangle is 6 cm.</p>
  <h3>Example 2: Perfect Square Division</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = 12]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 24]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{12^2}{24} = \\frac{144}{24} = 6]]')} /> cm. The squared leg divides evenly, so the answer is exact. The other projection here is 18 cm.</p>
  <h3>Example 3: Decimal Result</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = 7]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 14]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{7^2}{14} = \\frac{49}{14} = 3.5]]')} /> cm. A decimal result is normal: the projection need not be a whole number even when both inputs are. The other projection is 10.5 cm.</p>
  <h3>Example 4: Larger Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = 15]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{15^2}{25} = \\frac{225}{25} = 9]]')} /> m. Larger numbers change nothing about the method: square, then divide. The other projection is 16 m, which pairs with a leg of 20 m.</p>
  <h3>Example 5: Small Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = 30]]')} /> mm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 50]]')} /> mm, <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{30^2}{50} = \\frac{900}{50} = 18]]')} /> mm. Small measurements work the same way, and keeping the unit in millimeters throughout avoids any conversion. The other projection is 32 mm, which pairs with a leg of 40 mm.</p>
  <h3>Example 6: Fractional Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\frac{3}{4}]]')} /> ft and <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\frac{3}{2}]]')} /> ft, square the leg first: <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = \\frac{9}{16}]]')} /> square feet. Then <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{9/16}{3/2} = \\frac{9}{16} \\times \\frac{2}{3} = \\frac{18}{48} = \\frac{3}{8}]]')} /> ft. Dividing by a fraction means multiplying by its reciprocal, which is the step most students miss with fractional measurements.</p>
  <h3>Example 7: Different Units</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = 80]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 1]]')} /> m, convert first: 1 m equals 100 cm. Then <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{80^2}{100} = \\frac{6400}{100} = 64]]')} /> cm. Using 1 instead of 100 would have given 6400 cm, a projection far longer than the hypotenuse.</p>
  <h3>Example 8: Larger Decimal Values</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = 4.5]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 7.5]]')} /> m, <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{4.5^2}{7.5} = \\frac{20.25}{7.5} = 2.7]]')} /> m. Decimal inputs need the same care as whole numbers: square the full decimal, then divide. The other projection is 4.8 m, which pairs with a leg of 6 m.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 12: sec-projection-p -->
<section id="sec-projection-p" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Calculate Projection P From the Other Leg</h2>
  <p>Some problems ask for the other projection instead. The corresponding formula is the mirror image of the formula for q, with leg a in place of leg b:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[p = \\frac{a^2}{c}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[a = 6]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm. Then <span set:html={rich('[[p = \\frac{6^2}{10} = \\frac{36}{10} = 3.6]]')} /> cm. That matches the other projection of the 6-8-10 triangle from the earlier examples, where q was 6.4 cm and the two projections add to 10 cm.</p>
  <p>The formula uses a instead of b because each leg pairs with the projection next to its own vertex. Leg a touches vertex A, and p is the segment next to A. Entering b in this formula would return q, not p. The calculator on this page accepts only b and c, so use the <a href="/right-triangle-projection-p-calculator/">projection p calculator</a> when you need to find p from a and c.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 13: sec-relationship-pq -->
<section id="sec-relationship-pq" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Projection P and Projection Q</h2>
  <p>The two projections are the two parts of one straight side, so they add up to the whole hypotenuse:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[c = p + q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Rearranging gives <span set:html={rich('[[p = c - q]]')} />. With <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 6.4]]')} /> cm, the subtraction is <span set:html={rich('[[p = 10 - 6.4 = 3.6]]')} /> cm. The same subtraction works the other way: <span set:html={rich('[[q = c - p]]')} />. The <a href="/right-triangle-hypotenuse-from-projections-calculator/">hypotenuse from projections calculator</a> rebuilds c from p and q when the hypotenuse itself is unknown.</p>
  <p>Comparing p and q tells you about the shape of the triangle. When q equals p, the altitude lands at the midpoint of the hypotenuse and the two legs are equal, so the triangle is isosceles. When q is larger than p, leg b is longer than leg a, and when p is larger, leg a is longer. The longer projection always belongs to the longer leg.</p>
  <p>In the 6-8-10 triangle, q is 6.4 cm and p is 3.6 cm, so q is larger, and leg b at 8 cm is indeed longer than leg a at 6 cm.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('projection-q-vs-p.svg')} alt="Right triangle comparing projection q paired with leg b against projection p paired with leg a, with c equals 10, q equals 6.4, and p equals 3.6" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">q uses b (64 divided by 10 = 6.4) and p uses a (36 divided by 10 = 3.6).</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 14: sec-relationship-leg-b -->
<section id="sec-relationship-leg-b" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Projection Q and Leg B</h2>
  <p>The projection formula can be rearranged to find leg b instead of q. Starting from <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = c \\times q]]')} />, take the positive square root of both sides:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[b = \\sqrt{c \\times q}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Check it with the familiar numbers: <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 6.4]]')} /> cm. The product inside the root is <span set:html={rich('[[c \\times q = 10 \\times 6.4 = 64]]')} />, and <span set:html={rich('[[\\sqrt{64} = 8]]')} /> cm, which is the leg we started with.</p>
  <p>The connection is the geometric mean relationship: leg b is the geometric mean of the hypotenuse and its projection. That is why the leg appears squared in the theorem and why the square root appears when solving for the leg. For problems that start from q and c, the <a href="/right-triangle-leg-b-from-projection-calculator/">leg b from projection calculator</a> runs this rearranged formula directly.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 15: sec-relationship-altitude -->
<section id="sec-relationship-altitude" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Projection Q and Altitude</h2>
  <p>Projection q also connects to the altitude through a second geometric mean relationship. The altitude h is the geometric mean of the two projections:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h = \\sqrt{p \\times q}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Since <span set:html={rich('[[p = c - q]]')} />, the altitude can also be written using only q and c:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[h = \\sqrt{q(c - q)}]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Use <span set:html={rich('[[q = 6.4]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm. First find p: <span set:html={rich('[[p = 10 - 6.4 = 3.6]]')} /> cm. Then <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\sqrt{3.6 \\times 6.4} = \\sqrt{23.04} = 4.8]]')} /> cm. The altitude is shorter than either leg, as it must be.</p>
  <p>Projection q alone does not determine the altitude: two different triangles can share the same q with different hypotenuses. The complete hypotenuse, or the other projection, is always needed as well. The <a href="/right-triangle-altitude-from-projections-calculator/">altitude from projections calculator</a> handles that calculation when both projections are known.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 16: sec-pythagoras -->
<section id="sec-pythagoras" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Relationship Between Projection Q and the Pythagorean Theorem</h2>
  <p>The projection theorem and the Pythagorean theorem agree exactly, and the algebra shows why. Start from the two projection formulas, <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 = c \\times p]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = c \\times q]]')} />, and add them:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c \\times p + c \\times q]]]')} /></p>
  <p>Factoring out c from the right side gives <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = c(p + q)]]')} />. Since <span set:html={rich('[[p + q = c]]')} />, substitution yields the familiar result:</p>
  <p><span set:html={rich('[[[a^2 + b^2 = c^2]]]')} /></p>
  <p>A numerical check with the 6-8-10 triangle confirms it: <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = 36 + 64 = 100]]')} /> and <span set:html={rich('[[c^2 = 10^2 = 100]]')} />. The projection theorem splits the Pythagorean relationship into one piece per leg, and adding the pieces rebuilds it.</p>
  <p>That split is what makes the projection formulas useful: the Pythagorean theorem alone cannot find a projection from a leg and a hypotenuse, because it speaks about the legs together.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 17: sec-verify -->
<section id="sec-verify" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>How to Verify Your Projection Q Calculation</h2>
  <p>Four independent checks catch almost every error. Run at least one on every answer, and all four when the problem matters. The example below uses <span set:html={rich('[[b = 8]]')} /> cm, <span set:html={rich('[[c = 10]]')} /> cm, and <span set:html={rich('[[q = 6.4]]')} /> cm throughout.</p>
  <h3>Method 1: Projection Theorem Check</h3>
  <p>Verify <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{b^2}{c}]]')} /> directly. Here <span set:html={rich('[[\\frac{8^2}{10} = \\frac{64}{10} = 6.4]]')} /> cm, which matches the answer. If the recomputation differs, either the squaring or the division went wrong.</p>
  <h3>Method 2: Segment Addition</h3>
  <p>Find p by subtraction: <span set:html={rich('[[p = 10 - 6.4 = 3.6]]')} /> cm. Then check <span set:html={rich('[[p + q = 3.6 + 6.4 = 10]]')} /> cm, which equals c. A sum that misses c points to a subtraction slip.</p>
  <h3>Method 3: Pythagorean Theorem Check</h3>
  <p>Calculate leg a from p: <span set:html={rich('[[a = \\sqrt{10 \\times 3.6} = 6]]')} /> cm. Then check <span set:html={rich('[[a^2 + b^2 = 36 + 64 = 100 = 10^2]]')} />. This check tests the whole triangle, so it catches labeling errors the first check can miss. The <a href="/right-triangle-leg-a-from-projection-calculator/">leg a from projection calculator</a> runs the a step directly.</p>
  <h3>Method 4: Geometric Reasonableness</h3>
  <p>Confirm that q is positive, smaller than c, in the correct unit, and a hypotenuse segment rather than a perpendicular side. Here 6.4 cm is positive, smaller than 10 cm, in centimeters, and shorter than leg b at 8 cm.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 18: sec-properties -->
<section id="sec-properties" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Important Properties of Projection Q</h2>
  <p>Nine properties follow directly from <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{b^2}{c}]]')} />. Each one doubles as a sense check on a new answer.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Projection q is always shorter than the complete hypotenuse in a nondegenerate triangle, since q is only part of c.</li>
    <li>Increasing leg b while keeping c fixed increases q, because the squared leg in the numerator grows.</li>
    <li>Increasing c while keeping b fixed decreases q, because the denominator grows.</li>
    <li>If b and c are both multiplied by the same positive factor, q scales by that factor.</li>
    <li>If p equals q, both perpendicular legs are equal, and the triangle is an isosceles right triangle.</li>
    <li>Projection q is always positive in a valid nondegenerate triangle. Zero or negative values describe no real triangle.</li>
    <li>Projection q is a length, not an area. The squared leg is only an intermediate step, and dividing by c restores ordinary length units.</li>
    <li>Projection q is associated specifically with leg b. Using it with leg a answers a different question entirely.</li>
    <li>Swapping p and q changes which leg is associated with each projection, so the labels must stay matched to their vertices.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>When the altitude lands at the midpoint of the hypotenuse, the two legs must match, as the diagram below shows.</p>
  <figure class="not-prose my-6">
    <img src={imgSrc('projection-q-equal-vs-unequal.svg')} alt="Two right triangles side by side, one with equal projections p and q of 5 and one with unequal projections p equals 3.6 and q equals 6.4" class="mx-auto h-auto w-full max-w-xl my-8" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption class="mt-2 text-center text-sm text-on-surface-variant">Equal projections (p = q = 5) give equal legs, while unequal projections (p = 3.6, q = 6.4) give unequal legs.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<!-- Section 19: sec-mistakes -->
<section id="sec-mistakes" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Common Mistakes When Calculating Projection Q</h2>
  <p>Each mistake below is followed by its correction. Most trace back to labeling, not arithmetic.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Using leg a instead of leg b: entering a returns p. With a = 6 and c = 10, the result 3.6 cm is p, not q.</li>
    <li>Forgetting to square leg b: dividing b by c instead of b squared by c gives 0.8 cm instead of 6.4 cm.</li>
    <li>Dividing c by b squared instead of b squared by c: the quotient must be <span set:html={rich('[[b^2/c]]')} />, not <span set:html={rich('[[c/b^2]]')} />. With b = 8 and c = 10, the wrong order gives about 0.156, which has no valid unit.</li>
    <li>Confusing projection p with projection q: q sits next to vertex B and p next to vertex A. Label both segments before calculating.</li>
    <li>Treating q as the actual perpendicular leg: q is a segment of the hypotenuse, not side BC. In the 6-8-10 triangle, leg b is 8 cm while q is only 6.4 cm.</li>
    <li>Entering a leg value greater than or equal to c: no such triangle exists, since the hypotenuse is the longest side.</li>
    <li>Mixing centimeters and meters: convert first, as in Example 7, where 1 m became 100 cm.</li>
    <li>Forgetting that c represents the complete hypotenuse: using a projection or a leg as the denominator distorts the result.</li>
    <li>Rounding intermediate calculations too early: rounding the squared leg before dividing shifts the answer.</li>
    <li>Confusing b and b squared: 64 in the 6-8-10 example is a squared length, while 6.4 cm is the projection.</li>
    <li>Applying the formula to a triangle that is not right-angled: the projection theorem needs the altitude from a right angle.</li>
    <li>Treating q as the entire hypotenuse: q plus p equals c, so a result of c or more signals an error.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If an answer looks wrong, stop and relabel the diagram before recalculating. Most of the errors above vanish once p and q are clearly marked on the hypotenuse.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 20: sec-applications -->
<section id="sec-applications" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practical Applications of Right Triangle Projection Calculations</h2>
  <p>The most direct use is geometry homework and classroom work, where projection problems test whether students can label a diagram and apply <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{b^2}{c}]]')} /> instead of reaching for the Pythagorean theorem by habit. Similar-triangle exercises use the same setup, since the proof itself is an exercise in matching corresponding sides.</p>
  <p>Technical drawing uses the theorem when a right triangle must be reconstructed from partial measurements: if a drawing gives the hypotenuse and the foot of the altitude, the missing projection follows without remeasuring the shape. Teachers also use the setup as a bridge lesson between segment addition and similarity.</p>
  <p>One condition limits every application: the required right-triangle geometry must actually exist. The theorem describes exact geometric relationships, not approximate field measurements, so it never replaces careful surveying or professional engineering measurement.</p>
</section>

<!-- Section 21: sec-practice -->
<section id="sec-practice" class="mx-auto mt-12 w-full max-w-4xl section-shell prose-shell scroll-mt-24">
  <h2>Practice Problems With Answers</h2>
  <p>Try all six problems on paper before reading the answers. None of the values repeat the worked examples.</p>
  <h3>Problem 1: Basic Whole Numbers</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has leg <span set:html={rich('[[b = 9]]')} /> cm and hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 15]]')} /> cm. Find projection q.</p>
  <h3>Problem 2: Decimal Values</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = 1.5]]')} /> m and <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2.5]]')} /> m, find projection q.</p>
  <h3>Problem 3: Fractional Measurements</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[b = \\frac{5}{6}]]')} /> ft and <span set:html={rich('[[c = \\frac{5}{3}]]')} /> ft, find q as a fraction.</p>
  <h3>Problem 4: Unit Conversion</h3>
  <p>A leg measures <span set:html={rich('[[b = 80]]')} /> cm and the hypotenuse measures <span set:html={rich('[[c = 2]]')} /> m. Find q in centimeters.</p>
  <h3>Problem 5: Find p After Calculating q</h3>
  <p>A right triangle has hypotenuse <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm and leg <span set:html={rich('[[b = 15]]')} /> cm. Find q, then p.</p>
  <h3>Problem 6: Multi-Step With Projection q and Altitude h</h3>
  <p>Given <span set:html={rich('[[c = 25]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[b = 15]]')} /> cm, find q, then p, then the altitude h.</p>
  <h3>Practice Problem Answers</h3>
  <p>Answer 1: <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{9^2}{15} = \\frac{81}{15} = 5.4]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 2: <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{1.5^2}{2.5} = \\frac{2.25}{2.5} = 0.9]]')} /> m.</p>
  <p>Answer 3: <span set:html={rich('[[b^2 = \\frac{25}{36}]]')} />, so <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{25/36}{5/3} = \\frac{25}{36} \\times \\frac{3}{5} = \\frac{5}{12}]]')} /> ft.</p>
  <p>Answer 4: Convert 2 m to 200 cm, then <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{80^2}{200} = \\frac{6400}{200} = 32]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 5: <span set:html={rich('[[q = \\frac{15^2}{25} = \\frac{225}{25} = 9]]')} /> cm, then <span set:html={rich('[[p = 25 - 9 = 16]]')} /> cm.</p>
  <p>Answer 6: <span set:html={rich('[[q = 9]]')} /> cm and <span set:html={rich('[[p = 16]]')} /> cm, so <span set:html={rich('[[h = \\sqrt{9 \\times 16} = \\sqrt{144} = 12]]')} /> cm.</p>
</section>

